From: Andrew Berry <andrew@furrypaws.ca>
Subject: achieved-rate parser: reject a record whose rates are not numbers

The sibling of 010-reject-malformed-fping-samples.patch, on the other side of
the same shared pipe. ${main_fd} carries two unframed streams -- the
achieved-rate monitor's `SARS <dl> <ul>` and the pinger's reply lines -- so a
torn write can splice one onto the other. 010 hardened the pinger arm, where a
frankenline left the word `bytes` in the rtt field. This is the mirror image:
the splice landed in the SARS arm instead, and the record still had exactly
three fields, so upstream's field-count gate waved it through.

Reported against v3.2.2 with pinger_binary=fping:

    ERROR: 1786630033.560756 /usr/lib/cake-autorate/cake-autorate.sh: line 1191:
    ((: 390[1786630033.55848]: arithmetic syntax error: invalid arithmetic
    operator (error token is "[1786630033.55848]")

`390` is an upload rate in kbps; `[1786630033.55848]` is an fping `--timestamp`
prefix (fping prints five decimals) welded onto it. The fused value was stored
into achieved_rate_kbps[ul] and reached the `((...))` load comparison on line
1191, which cannot parse it.

BOTH fields are reachable, so both are gated. Seven crashes over three hours on
the reporting router were this one error and nothing else, split across the two
halves of the same comparison: line 1191 with `459[...]` / `390[...]` (the ul
rate, command[2]) and line 1181 with `0[...]` five times over (the dl rate,
command[1]). The splice therefore lands at no fixed offset in the record -- it
is a torn write, not a parser quirk -- and the fused timestamp is consistently
~2.3 ms older than the abort, i.e. the pinger line it came from was still in
flight when the record was read.

The failure mode is NOT 010's. There the abort is a clean exit that procd does
not respawn. Here bash writes a diagnostic to stderr, and upstream's
intercept_stderr() answers ANY stderr line with `log_msg ERROR` followed by
`kill $$` -- so the daemon dies, procd respawns it, and the router logs a crash
loop rather than a silent stop:

    Stopped cake-autorate with PID: 25821 and config: /etc/cake-autorate/config.primary.sh
    procd: Instance cake-autorate::primary s in a crash loop 7 crashes, 78 seconds since last crash

So gate the two fields the arithmetic consumes, in the same style as 010, and
drop the record when either is not a plain integer. A rate sample is
disposable: monitor_achieved_rates emits the next one within
monitor_achieved_rates_interval_ms.

Neither this nor 010 fixes the underlying interleaving -- the pipe is still
shared and unframed -- they stop it from being fatal. Upstream is unfixed in
v3.2.2 (still the newest tag) and in master at 3.3.0-PRERELEASE: the SARS arm
is byte-identical there, and intercept_stderr still calls kill $$. Drop this
patch on the upstream bump that first ships a fix for it.

--- a/cake-autorate.sh
+++ b/cake-autorate.sh
@@ -1165,7 +1165,24 @@

 		# Set download and upload achieved rates
 		SARS)
-			if ((${#command[@]} == 3))
+			# Upstream trusts every 3-field line the fifo yields. That is not
+			# safe: ${main_fd} is shared by the achieved-rate monitor and the
+			# pinger, and a torn write can splice a pinger timestamp onto a rate
+			# while leaving the field COUNT at 3, which is all this gate checks.
+			# Both fields then land in achieved_rate_kbps and are compared with
+			# ((...)) a few lines below, where a non-numeric token is a bash
+			# error -- and intercept_stderr turns any stderr line into kill $$.
+			#
+			# Glob class checks, not [[ =~ ]], matching the fping gate in the
+			# pinger arm: an ERE recompiles on every record. -n as well as the
+			# class, because an empty field passes *[!0-9]* and would make the
+			# comparison a bare (( > thr )). A rejected record is dropped,
+			# silently and with no new control flow: the next one is along within
+			# monitor_achieved_rates_interval_ms, and the stale rates it leaves
+			# in place are the ones the load logic already had.
+			if ((${#command[@]} == 3)) &&
+				[[ -n ${command[1]} && ${command[1]} != *[!0-9]* ]] &&
+				[[ -n ${command[2]} && ${command[2]} != *[!0-9]* ]]
 			then
 				achieved_rate_kbps[dl]=${command[1]} achieved_rate_kbps[ul]=${command[2]} achieved_rate_updated[dl]=1 achieved_rate_updated[ul]=1

